WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.340
A symbolic link is a filesystem entry that directs path resolution to another path.

2
00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:13.560
That target may be absolute or relative, and the meaning of the link can depend on where the link is located.

3
00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:20.940
For a C program, the important point is that a path can include components whose final object is not obvious from the text alone.

4
00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:29.140
Hard links are different at a high level: they create additional names for the same underlying file object where the filesystem permits them.

5
00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:34.280
They can matter for review because the same object may have more than one directory entry.

6
00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:40.460
The program should reason about object identity and permissions, not only the spelling of one path.

7
00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:44.900
Relative paths depend on current working directory context.

8
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:53.080
A service, scheduled job, test harness, container, or command-line tool may run from a directory the developer did not expect.

9
00:00:53.180 --> 00:01:01.740
Absolute paths remove that specific ambiguity, but they still require trust decisions about directories, permissions, and object identity.

10
00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:04.820
Path resolution crosses filesystem state.

11
00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:12.440
Directories may be replaced, mounted, renamed, or changed by other code depending on permissions and platform behavior.

12
00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:19.100
Filesystem boundaries and mount behavior are platform-specific enough that portable C code should avoid assuming one uniform model.

13
00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:23.460
The difference between a path string and an opened file object is central.

14
00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:27.080
A path string describes how the program might reach an object later.

15
00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:33.500
An opened descriptor or handle represents the object the program is actually using now.

16
00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:38.960
Safe designs reduce repeated trust in strings and validate the object in use.

17
00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:48.440
Reviewers should ask what the code trusts: the path text, the directory that contains it, the platform's resolution rules, or the object actually opened.

18
00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:52.060
Clear answers make link behavior easier to reason about.
